<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mont-Saint-Michel</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mont-Saint-Michel is a tidal island off the coast of Normandy, near the border with Brittany, crowned by a Benedictine abbey dedicated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkhangelsk">Archangel</a> Michael. A sanctuary was founded there in 708 and a Benedictine community in 966 under the dukes of Normandy. Its fortified abbey resisted English sieges during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>, and the site is now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mont-saint-michel.jpg" alt="Mont-Saint-Michel" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Amaustan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mont-Saint-Michel_vu_du_ciel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The island lies about a kilometre off the north-western coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, at the mouth of the Couesnon River near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avranches">Avranches</a>. It is a granite outcrop with a circumference of about 960 metres and a highest point 92 metres above sea level. Tides vary by roughly 14 metres, and medieval pilgrims crossing the flats nicknamed it St. Michael in peril of the sea. It was accessible to pilgrims at low tide, and defensible because the incoming tide could strand, drive off or drown attackers on foot.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before the first monastic establishment of the 8th century the island was called Mont Tombe. According to a legend, the archangel Michael appeared in 708 to Aubert, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avranches">Avranches</a>, and told him to build a church on the rock. In 933 William I Longsword annexed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotentin-peninsula">Cotentin Peninsula</a> from the weakened Duchy of Brittany, which made the mount definitively part of Normandy. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayeux-tapestry">Bayeux Tapestry</a> shows it. In 1067 the monastery supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a>'s claim to the English throne, and he rewarded it with English properties, including St Michael's Mount in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>.</p><p>In the 11th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-ii-duke-of-normandy">Richard II, Duke of Normandy</a>, chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-of-volpiano">William of Volpiano</a> as builder. He designed the Romanesque abbey church with its transept crossing at the top of the mount, and many underground crypts and chapels were built to carry the weight. In 1204 Guy of Thouars besieged the mount, burned the village and massacred its people, and the fire spread to the abbey. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france">Philip Augustus</a> then offered Abbot Jordan a grant to rebuild in the new Gothic style. Abbot Pierre le Roy, from 1386 to 1410, added fortifications. English forces besieged the island unsuccessfully from 1423 and again from 1433, under Thomas Scales, 7th Baron Scales.</p><p>The abbey's prestige as a pilgrimage centre declined after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> scarcely any monks remained. It became a prison, first for clerical opponents of the republic and then for political prisoners. A campaign including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-hugo">Victor Hugo</a> for its restoration began by 1836. The prison closed in 1863, the abbey was classified a historic monument in 1874, and the architect Édouard Corroyer spent fifteen years restoring the part known as la Merveille. German soldiers occupied the island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and it was liberated on 1 August 1944.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The mount and its bay were inscribed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 1979, and over 60 buildings in the commune are protected as historical monuments. A community of the Monastic Fraternities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> has lived there since 2001.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mont-Saint-Michel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The dukes restored Jumièges and Fécamp, Benedictine monks were installed at <b>Mont-Saint-Michel</b> in 966, and William of Volpiano reformed Fécamp from 1001.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
